course: dictionary
lesson: 11232
title: Wordplay: playing with words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Wordplay is when someone plays with words on purpose to make you smile, think or groan. Writers, comedians and poets all use it. Puns, rhymes and words with two meanings are all kinds of wordplay.
explain: Look at this: «Rap uses rhythm, rhyme and wordplay, so it is a kind of poetry.» Wordplay sits in a list with rhythm and rhyme, so it is one of the tools a rap writer uses with words. The sentence shows you what the word means.
explain: Wordplay often depends on one word having two meanings. When a joke makes you pause, ask yourself: is a word being used in two ways? If it is, you have spotted the wordplay.
fact: Wordplay means a clever or funny use of words.
fact: A pun is one kind of wordplay: a word with two meanings, used to make a joke.
fact: Rhymes, riddles, tongue twisters and spoonerisms are also kinds of wordplay.
fact: Roald Dahl and Lewis Carroll use a lot of wordplay in their writing.
fact: Wordplay is uncountable, so you say "some wordplay", not "a wordplay" or "wordplays".
exercise: Which of these is an example of wordplay? | A plain weather report | A list of shopping items | *A pun, where one word has two meanings and makes a joke | A sum with numbers only
exercise: «Rap uses rhythm, rhyme and wordplay, so it is a kind of poetry.» Find and copy the word that means a clever or funny use of words. | rhyme | poetry | rhythm | *wordplay
exercise: Priya says wordplay is just spelling words correctly. What is the best answer? | Reading very quickly | *A clever or funny use of words, such as puns or rhymes | Counting the letters in a word | Spelling words correctly in a test
test: Roald Dahl and Lewis Carroll are famous for using a lot of what? | Maths | Sport | *Wordplay | Weather
test: Which sentence uses wordplay correctly? | *We enjoyed some wordplay in the poem. | We enjoyed a wordplays in the poem. | We enjoyed wordplays in the poem.
exam: Which of these is the best example of wordplay? | A list of numbers | A long walk to school | *A joke where "bark" means both a dog's noise and a tree's outer layer | A sentence about the weather
